  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Carcinoid tumors are neuroendocrine tumors arising from amine precursor uptake and decarboxylation (APUD) cells.",
              "pdf_page": 306,
              "printed_page": 224,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Carcinoids originate from enterochromaffin and enterochromaffin-like cells of the gastrointestinal tract, but can be found in the liver, gall bladder, pancreas, tracheobronchial tree and genitourinary system due to embryologic origins.",
              "pdf_page": 306,
              "printed_page": 224,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Carcinoids may secrete amines such as histamine, serotonin, prostaglandin, and peptides such as bradykinins and tachykinins.",
              "pdf_page": 306,
              "printed_page": 224,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "In humans, secretion causes “carcinoid syndrome.” This occurs in approximately 5–10% of patients with metastatic carcinoid tumors. Hepatic degradation of excess amines, notably seratonin, is bypassed, causing clinical signs. Human carcinoid syndrome is characterized by flushing, abdominal pain, diarrhea, bronchospasm, and cyanosis. This syndrome has not been reported in small animals, although a recent report describes a dog with episodic collapse and melena and an ileocecal carcinoid.",
              "pdf_page": 306,
              "printed_page": 224,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Morbidity and mortality are more often a function of tumor size and gastrointestinal blockage.",
              "pdf_page": 306,
              "printed_page": 224,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Primary carcinoid tumors in dogs have been reported in the stomach, small intestine, colon, lung, gallbladder, and liver. In cats, carcinoids have been found in the stomach, small intestine, liver, and heart.",
              "pdf_page": 306,
              "printed_page": 224,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
